  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.common_neighbors - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.common_neighbors.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the number of common neighbors for node pairs.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the number of common neighbors for node pairs.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.common_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the number of common neighbors for node pairs.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the number of common neighbors for node pairs.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L30240-L30362){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.common_neighbors(ebunch=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Common neighbor count](../assets/graphs/common-neighbors.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Nodes B and E are outlined in the accent color and their two shared neighbors C and D are filled in accent. The card shows the intersection of the two neighborhoods, C and D, giving a common-neighbor count of 2 for the pair.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nFor each pair of nodes `(u, v)`, counts how many nodes are neighbors of both `u` and `v`.\nCommon neighbors is a simple but effective link prediction measure: the more neighbors\ntwo unconnected nodes share, the more likely an edge between them is to appear (or to be\nmissing from incomplete data). The graph is not modified.\n\nTypical uses:\n\n- \"people you may know\" style recommendations (friends of friends)\n- ranking candidate links when completing a partially observed network\n- a fast baseline to compare against the degree-aware scores `jaccard_coefficient()`,\n  `adamic_adar_index()`, and `resource_allocation_index()`\n\nThe measure is intended for undirected graphs. On a directed graph, neighbors are the\n*outgoing* neighbors, so the score counts shared successors. When `ebunch=` is `None`,\nevery pair of non-adjacent nodes is scored, which is quadratic in the number of nodes\nand can produce a very long list on large graphs; pass `ebunch=` to score only the pairs\nof interest.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[ebunch]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[tuple[Union[int, str], Union[int, str]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   List of `(u, v)` node pairs to score. Each node can be given as a node ID (`int`) or\n    label (`str`). Pairs are scored even if `u` and `v` are already connected, and they\n    are returned in the order given. If `None` (the default), all pairs of nodes that are\n    not already connected (in either direction) are scored, each unordered pair once,\n    smaller node ID first, in node ID order. A node that doesn't exist raises a `ValueError`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[tuple[int, int, int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of `(u, v, count)` tuples where count is the number of common neighbors. Nodes\n    are reported as node IDs, even if labels were passed in `ebunch=`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If a node in `ebunch=` does not exist in the graph.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nFor each candidate pair $(u, v)$, the score is simply $|N(u) \\cap N(v)|$, computed\nin $O(k)$ per pair on undirected simple graphs (where $k$ is the average degree).\nDespite its crudeness, the metric remains a competitive baseline for link prediction\nin social networks with high clustering: the more mutual friends two people share,\nthe more likely they are to befriend each other. It ignores degree entirely, so hubs\ndominate; the Jaccard, Adamic-Adar, and resource-allocation variants correct for that\nin different ways.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~jaccard_coefficient`) \n\n:   The count divided by the size of the two neighborhoods together.\n\n[](`~resource_allocation_index`) \n\n:   A count that weighs each shared neighbor by one over its degree.\n\n[](`~cn_soundarajan_hopcroft`) \n\n:   A count with a bonus for shared neighbors in the same community.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`A` and `D` aren't joined but share `B` and `C`, and `B` and `E` share `D`. Each result is\na pair of node IDs and its count:\n\n::: {#023647a6 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\")]\n)\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\npairs = [(\"A\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"E\")]\n\n[(label(u), label(v), n) for u, v, n in graph.common_neighbors(pairs)]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[('A', 'D', 2), ('B', 'E', 1)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWithout pairs, every pair of nodes that aren't joined is scored:\n\n::: {#01d8efd7 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nlen(graph.common_neighbors())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n5\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Who became friends\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. A year later, in `friends(snapshot=2)`, eight more pairs had\nbecome friends. Of the pairs who weren't friends yet, the four with three friends in\ncommon all became friends. The next four, with two, didn't, and three of them, like Ben\nand Jules, share only Hugo and Tara, the couple whose friends span both clubs:\n\n::: {#5d221461 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nlater = ec.friends(snapshot=2)\nlabel = friends.get_label_from_id\ntop = sorted(friends.common_neighbors(), key=lambda t: -t[2])[:8]\n\n[\n    (label(u), label(v), n, later.has_edge(label(u), label(v)))\n    for u, v, n in top\n]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n[('Ada', 'Dev', 3, True),\n ('Gwen', 'Jules', 3, True),\n ('Iris', 'Lou', 3, True),\n ('Mara', 'Pablo', 3, True),\n ('Ben', 'Ella', 2, False),\n ('Ben', 'Jules', 2, False),\n ('Ben', 'Oona', 2, False),\n ('Ben', 'Wes', 2, False)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe four new friendships, in the second snapshot:\n\n::: {#7a81ea70 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nlater.plot(\n    highlight=[(label(u), label(v)) for u, v, _ in top[:4]],\n    color_by=\"club\", label_by=\"label\", seed=1,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa